# Narrow passwordless sudo for the operator account (pi5_aide), so a configuration
# change has a delivery path of its own instead of re-running the provisioning.
# Install to /etc/sudoers.d/beacon-configs with mode 0440; validate: sudo visudo -c.
#
# This adds no capability. pi5_aide already holds (ALL:ALL) ALL on this host; the
# line below removes a password prompt from something the account can already do.
# That prompt is the whole problem: it cannot be answered by anything scripted, so
# `make init` was the only delivery path for configs/ and configuration fixes sat
# in git without taking effect.
#
# Why the grant names a script and not `install`: with a parameterised `install`
# the caller chooses the destination, which is passwordless root by construction.
# /usr/local/sbin/beacon-install-configs is root:root 0755, so pi5_aide cannot
# edit the source-to-destination table it carries, and the reachable set of paths
# is fixed when `make init` puts the script there. The script installs neither
# itself nor this file, so neither can be rewritten through the grant either.
#
# The argument lists are spelled out rather than left open, which means adding a
# flag to the script also means updating this file and re-running `make init`.
# That friction is intended: it keeps the grant readable as exactly what it allows.
# The CI account (github_aide) is deliberately not granted this — see
# configs/beacon-deploy.sudoers for the boundary that keeps a leaked deploy key
# confined to /opt/beacon/{artifacts,bin}.
pi5_aide ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/beacon-install-configs "", \
                              /usr/local/sbin/beacon-install-configs --dry-run
